{
  "concept": "In ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, the enthalpy change sets the y-intercept (spontaneity as T→0) and the entropy change sets the slope, so only when ΔH and ΔS share a sign does raising the temperature flip a process between spontaneous and nonspontaneous at the point where ΔG = 0.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "vertical axis labeled 'Free energy' with a single tick mark at 0",
    "horizontal axis labeled 'Increasing temperature (K)'",
    "gray dashed horizontal line at ΔG = 0 spanning the plot",
    "pink shaded upper half-plane labeled 'Nonspontaneous' with 'ΔG > 0' printed beside the axis",
    "green shaded lower half-plane labeled 'Spontaneous' with 'ΔG < 0' printed beside the axis",
    "red line rising to the upper right, starting high in the pink region, labeled 'ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0'",
    "green line falling to the lower right, starting in the green region, labeled 'ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0'",
    "yellow line sloping downward from the pink region across the zero line, labeled 'ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0'",
    "yellow line sloping upward from the green region across the zero line, labeled 'ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0'",
    "vertical temperature cursor line (movable)",
    "highlighted point marker on each of the four lines at the cursor's x-position"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "temperature",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Temperature (K)",
      "range": [0, 100, 1],
      "default": 15,
      "teaches": "Dragging the temperature cursor moves each line's current ΔG point up or down its line; the two same-sign (yellow) lines cross the ΔG = 0 line at their own temperatures and flip between the spontaneous and nonspontaneous regions, while the opposite-sign lines never cross."
    },
    {
      "id": "signCombination",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sign combination",
      "options": ["All four", "ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0", "ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0", "ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0", "ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0"],
      "default": "All four",
      "teaches": "Isolating one pair shows that ΔH fixes where the line starts at T = 0 (the intercept, hence the low-temperature side) and ΔS fixes the slope sign (whether ΔG rises or falls as T increases) — together these two signs determine if and where spontaneity reverses."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Four sign combinations",
      "narration": "At low temperature all four lines sit on the side set by their ΔH: the red (ΔH>0, ΔS<0) line is nonspontaneous and the green (ΔH<0, ΔS>0) line is spontaneous, and neither will ever cross zero because their slopes push them further away.",
      "state": { "signCombination": "All four", "temperature": 15 }
    },
    {
      "title": "ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0: heat-driven spontaneity",
      "narration": "Isolating this yellow line shows it starts in the pink (nonspontaneous) region because ΔH > 0 sets a positive intercept, but its negative slope (−ΔS < 0) carries it downward until it crosses ΔG = 0 — above that temperature the process becomes spontaneous.",
      "state": { "signCombination": "ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0", "temperature": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0: cold-driven spontaneity",
      "narration": "The other yellow line starts in the green (spontaneous) region because ΔH < 0, but its positive slope (−ΔS > 0) lifts it until it crosses ΔG = 0 — here raising the temperature destroys spontaneity, the mirror image of the previous case.",
      "state": { "signCombination": "ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0", "temperature": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the temperature",
      "narration": "With all four lines shown, drag the temperature cursor from low to high: the red and green lines stay on their original sides, while the two yellow lines each flip shading exactly at their own ΔG = 0 crossing — the temperature at which ΔH = TΔS.",
      "state": { "signCombination": "All four", "temperature": 70 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "ΔH sets the intercept and ΔS sets the slope of ΔG versus T, so a process reverses spontaneity with temperature only when ΔH and ΔS have the same sign — and the reversal happens exactly where the line crosses ΔG = 0.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Standard 2D axes: vertical 'Free energy' with one tick at 0, horizontal 'Increasing temperature (K)'. A gray dashed horizontal line at ΔG = 0 divides the plot; fill the upper half a soft pink with the label 'Nonspontaneous' and 'ΔG > 0' near the axis, and the lower half a soft green with 'Spontaneous' and 'ΔG < 0'. Draw four straight lines all originating at the left edge: a red line starting high in the pink zone and rising right (label 'ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0'); a green line starting in the green zone and falling right (label 'ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0'); a yellow line starting in pink and sloping down across the zero line (label 'ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0'); a yellow line starting in green and sloping up across the zero line (label 'ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0'). Angle each ΔH/ΔS label parallel to its line. Add a thin vertical cursor line controlled by the temperature slider and a glowing dot on each visible line at the cursor's x; tint each dot green when below zero and red/pink when above so the spontaneity flip is visible at a glance. When a single sign combination is selected via the toggle, fade the other three lines to faint gray and bold the chosen line, its label, and its zero-crossing point."
}

